## WHAT THE WORK IS (the order is the order)
### Part 1: MPSCALC-339 (commit 1)
Find the input that reaches the `Consumables` null walk at BOTH call sites (a click rate's `supplies.consumables`, deal `usage.supplies.consumables`) on all three routes (create, patch, duplicate), or prove none does. The measurement is in-process and has two parts: (a) **the reach table** (print it in STATUS): for each of `clickRates[i].supplies.consumables` and `usage.supplies.consumables`, one probe body with every `required` member present and N null entries (N = 1, 60, 100,000), the status, the code, the errors list length and the answer size in bytes, through the existing raw patch helper and the DevStub seller (and what the same body gets as a caller WITHOUT the internal-metrics permission, if the host offers one: say which); plus a body with the gate's shape (consumables only) as the CONTROL that reproduces the 234-byte refusal the gate saw (a control that can fail: if it does not reproduce, say so before building on it). (b) **The verdict, one of:** REACHABLE (a probe body reaches `Consumables`; its answer grows with N, which is the case by the code reading at `:287-290`) -> cap it; or UNREACHABLE (every body that carries a null consumable is refused before `Validate` reaches the walk) -> a written proof in STATUS with the cite (file, line, the refusal's code and text, the probe that shows it) PLUS the pin rows below.
- **If REACHABLE, the cap (D5):** the walk adds at most `MaxWalkEntriesListed` "must not be null." entries **in all across the draft** (both call sites and every click rate: one walk, one budget, like the null-entries cap `MaxNullEntriesListed` that spans every list), counted in entries, the first ones in the order the lists are checked; the count error for a list over `MaxConsumables` and the "is required" entry are unchanged and not counted against it. A per-list cap is NOT enough: `Count` at `:109` does not stop the click-rate loop, so 100,000 click rates with one null each would still list 100,000 entries. Rows (default 4, cap 2 more, D2): the 100,000-null row for a click rate with the answer size asserted as a bound and not a figure (entries <= count error + 20), the same for `usage`, a row of many click rates with one null each (the all-lists budget), and a row where non-null errors AFTER the walk (a bad `partNumber`, a bad `description`) still list in full. The unreachable pin rows are not written in this branch.
- **If UNREACHABLE:** the proof is a STATUS section (cite the refusal at its line, show the probes). Add the pin rows (default 2, one per call site): a body with every required member and a null consumable is refused with the exact observed problem (status, code, detail) and **stores nothing** (revision unchanged), so a later change that lets the walk be reached goes red. Their expected values are the ones YOUR probe printed at the unfixed base. If the unreachability depends on the caller's permission, the row says so in its name and uses the permission's own helper; if no helper exists, STOP with one question.
- **Part 1 STOP rules:** a cap that needs a changed or new string literal (the entry text is the existing sentence; no new sentence is allowed, D3); a reach that needs a path outside Yours; or a probe that needs a hosted host. One question, quote what you found.

### Part 2: MPSCALC-338 (commit 2)
**Step 3 first (the docs measurement, before any product edit):** re-read `docs/API.md` and `docs/api/openapi.json` at your base, `gh api -X GET` raw (not the local checkout; it is stale), and print for each of `one error per`, `first 20`, `bomLines`, `errors[]`, `name each value` the count and the line numbers with a control (`zzz-no-such-string-7f3a` = 0 in both). Say in STATUS which sentences, if any, say or imply that every bad BOM line gets its own entry, and your verdict: DOCS NOT NEEDED (with the evidence) or DOCS NEEDED (STOP, THE DOCS STOP above).

**The cap (the property, then the closed list).** The property (D6): *at 100,000 lines the answer for each of the five fields (line number, catalogue key, manual item id with its names-exactly-one entry, quantity, note) holds at most 20 entries of that field, the first ones in line order; the count error is unchanged; no field's budget is spent by an entry of any other field, any other check or any other walk; every line counts, whatever its line number; every other error of the draft is still listed in full.* **One edit variant of the counter bookkeeping that breaks any clause of it, with no committed row going red, is a survivor.** Decisions to make with evidence (record each in STATUS under DESIGN):
- **The boundary is exact: 20, never 21 or 19.** `Text` can add TWO entries for one note (length and control character), and a line with a catalogue key and a bad manual item id adds TWO (the id entry and the names-exactly-one entry). A counter that adds `errors.Count - before` after the line's checks (the section id counter's shape) can overshoot to 21 when the 20th slot falls in the middle of such a pair. Acceptance reads "at most 21 entries for each of five fields (the count error and the first twenty listed)": **a field lists exactly 20 when it has 20 or more to list, even if that cuts a pair.** Measure it at the unfixed base with a probe (a note of 201 characters with a control character on every line after one single-entry line), then build the counter so the 20th is the last.
- **Manual item id and names-exactly-one are ONE field with ONE counter** (default D7): the ticket and the gate read them as one walk ("manual item id and the names-exactly-one check", the ticket's Where; 200,001 entries at 100,000 lines), and the acceptance is per field. A separate counter for the names-exactly-one entry would let that field list 40. The cost, stated: a line with neither a catalogue key nor a manual item id (only the names-exactly-one entry) spends the same budget as a bad manual item id. Q-3 asks Tuesday; the gate may call a shared budget across two checks the g01 shape: pin both behaviours with rows (R10) so that either reading is a measured one.
- **Five new counters plus the section id counter, all independent.** No new public constant is required (reuse `MaxWalkEntriesListed`); update the doc comment at `:24-28` so it describes which checks the constant bounds, in words, with no key. The helper shape (a small private method or struct, or six locals) is your choice; record why.

